+# Shita backend and application audit
+
+**Snapshot:** `main` at `b6b751282b843623f9577ae8baf6705247e4d495` (2026-08-20).  
+**Review date:** 2026-10-27.  
+**Method:** Static review of the repository source and configuration. No dependency installation, build, test, deployment, credential use, or production-data access was performed.
+
+This report is based on the checked-in source, not README claims. The [client operation inventory](CLIENT_OPERATION_INVENTORY.md) records detected GraphQL operation labels, root fields, and locations. All GitHub source references are pinned to the snapshot commit.
+
+## Executive finding
+
+The repository contains the requested client surfaces, including an admin dashboard and a customer web storefront. It also contains a newer Apollo/Mongoose backend, but that backend is **not a compatible, secure end-to-end service for the checked-in clients**. Their GraphQL contracts diverge substantially; many client root fields are absent, several declared backend fields are placeholders, and core order mutations are missing or have incompatible arguments and return shapes.
+
+There is order-related code, but the complete customer → restaurant → driver → delivered workflow is not implemented as one usable, authorized contract. The current resolver permits arbitrary authenticated users to perform important order actions. Payments are not verified, push delivery is not implemented, and repository credentials require owner-side revocation and rotation before any live use.
+
+## Applications and backend components
+
+| Surface | Source directory | What source exists |
+|---|---|---|
+| Customer mobile | `shita-multivendor-app/` | Expo / React Native application with Android and iOS configuration. |
+| Restaurant app | `shita-multivendor-store/` | Separate Expo / React Native restaurant/store application. |
+| Delivery-driver app | `shita-multivendor-rider/` | Separate Expo / React Native rider application. |
+| Admin web | `shita-multivendor-admin/` | Next.js dashboard with super-admin and restaurant/vendor areas, including order dispatch, restaurants, riders, users, and configuration pages. |
+| Customer web storefront | `shita-multivendor-web/` | Separate Next.js customer storefront. |
+| API backend | `shita-multivendor-backend/` | TypeScript Apollo GraphQL server with Mongoose models and MongoDB connection code. |
+| Infrastructure scaffold | `Terraform/` | AWS ECS, API Gateway, RDS/PostgreSQL with PostGIS, Redis, SQS, S3, secrets, WAF, and related Terraform resources. This is infrastructure source, not evidence of a deployed or connected service. |
+
+Arabic and English localization resources exist in multiple clients. That establishes localization assets, not complete translation coverage, layout/RTL correctness, or an end-to-end language test.
+
+### Backend modules actually present
+
+| Module | Source | Implemented content |
+|---|---|---|
+| Authentication/users | `src/graphql/resolvers/auth.ts`, `src/models/User.ts` | Login/account creation, profile update, password change, OTP storage/verification, push-token storage. Email/SMS sending, forgot/reset-password resolvers, and user listing have gaps or weak controls. |
+| Restaurants | `src/graphql/resolvers/restaurant.ts`, `src/models/Restaurant.ts` | Restaurant list/detail/nearby/top-rated queries and a limited set of create/edit/delete/delivery-area mutations. Some ownership checks exist. |
+| Orders | `src/graphql/resolvers/order.ts`, `src/models/Order.ts` | Order creation, listing, detail, generic status update, rider assignment, cancellation, review, and in-process events. Important authorization, pricing, transition, and client-contract controls are missing. |
+| Menu/catalog | `src/models/Food.ts`, `Category.ts`, `Addon.ts` | Models exist. Resolver registration returns empty lists or throws “Not implemented” for several corresponding operations. |
+| Addresses | `src/models/Address.ts` | Model exists; address mutations in the registered resolver map throw “Not implemented”. |
+| Coupons/zones | `src/models/Coupon.ts`, `Zone.ts` | Models and some schema types exist; resolver registration contains empty/stub reads and unimplemented writes. |
+| Runtime/configuration | `src/index.ts`, `src/config/`, `src/middleware/auth.ts` | Apollo standalone HTTP entry point, Mongoose connection, environment parsing, JWT authentication, and role helper functions. No included REST router or WebSocket server is configured by the entry point. |
+| Infrastructure | `Terraform/*.tf` | AWS resource definitions, separate from the current MongoDB service connection. |
+
+## Client-to-backend contract coverage
+
+The client modules use a large legacy GraphQL vocabulary. A static scan found the following distinct root fields and compared their names with the backend GraphQL SDL:
+
+| Client | Distinct root fields detected | Not declared in backend SDL |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| Customer mobile | 59 | 32 |
+| Restaurant mobile | 48 | 30 |
+| Rider mobile | 72 | 46 |
+| Admin web | 155 | 119 |
+| Customer web | 58 | 39 |
+
+These counts are per-client static matches, not a count of unique features across the product. A field marked “Declared” in the inventory may still have an incompatible signature, incompatible selected fields, a placeholder resolver, or no authorization. The inventory is not a live GraphQL validation.
+
+Examples of confirmed differences:
+
+* The clients call `configuration`; the backend exposes `getConfiguration`.
+* Restaurant clients call `acceptOrder(_id, time)` and `orderPickedUp(_id)`; neither field is in the backend schema.
+* Rider clients call `assignOrder(id)` and `updateOrderStatusRider(id, status)`; neither field is in the backend schema.
+* The admin dispatch client calls `updateStatus(id, orderStatus)` and `assignRider(id, riderId)`. The backend has `updateOrderStatus(id, status, reason)` and `assignRider(orderId, riderId)`; the latter has a different required argument name.
+* Customer/restaurant/rider clients call `placeOrder` with separate arguments and an array of legacy `OrderInput` values. The backend accepts a single `orderInput: OrderInput!` object with a different item/address structure.
+* Client order selections request properties such as `status`, `orderDate`, `expectedTime`, `preparationTime`, `completionTime`, `assignedAt`, and legacy item fields. The backend `Order` / `OrderItem` types do not expose that complete shape.
+
+Important source locations:
+
+* [Backend root schema and types](https://github.com/ferasshita/Shita-Multivendor-Food-Delivery/blob/b6b751282b843623f9577ae8baf6705247e4d495/shita-multivendor-backend/src/graphql/typeDefs/index.ts)
+* [Backend resolver registration and placeholders](https://github.com/ferasshita/Shita-Multivendor-Food-Delivery/blob/b6b751282b843623f9577ae8baf6705247e4d495/shita-multivendor-backend/src/graphql/resolvers/index.ts)
+* [Store order operations](https://github.com/ferasshita/Shita-Multivendor-Food-Delivery/blob/b6b751282b843623f9577ae8baf6705247e4d495/shita-multivendor-store/lib/api/graphql/mutation/order/index.ts)
+* [Rider order operations](https://github.com/ferasshita/Shita-Multivendor-Food-Delivery/blob/b6b751282b843623f9577ae8baf6705247e4d495/shita-multivendor-rider/lib/apollo/mutations/order.mutation.ts)
+* [Admin dispatch operations](https://github.com/ferasshita/Shita-Multivendor-Food-Delivery/blob/b6b751282b843623f9577ae8baf6705247e4d495/shita-multivendor-admin/lib/api/graphql/mutations/dispatch/index.ts)
+* [Customer web order operations](https://github.com/ferasshita/Shita-Multivendor-Food-Delivery/blob/b6b751282b843623f9577ae8baf6705247e4d495/shita-multivendor-web/lib/api/graphql/mutations/orders/index.ts)
+
+## Order lifecycle: what source actually does
+
+| Step | Source finding | Result |
+|---|---|---|
+| Customer creates order | `placeOrder` requires authentication and checks that the restaurant and each food item are active/available. It does not check that each food belongs to the selected restaurant. Client-supplied amounts, tax, discount, delivery charge, and total are stored instead of recalculated from trusted server-side prices. | A create path exists, but it is not safe for real orders or payments. |
+| Restaurant accepts | The store app calls `acceptOrder`; the included backend schema has no such mutation. The backend has a generic status mutation that can set `ACCEPTED`, but it does not verify restaurant ownership or that the previous status permits acceptance. | Not implemented as a compatible, authorized restaurant action. |
+| Driver assignment | Backend `assignRider(orderId, riderId)` checks that the selected user has role `RIDER`, then assigns that rider and sets `ASSIGNED`. It does not require an admin/vendor/rider role for the caller, check restaurant scope, rider availability/zone, or enforce an allowed source status. Client operations use incompatible signatures/names. | A database update exists, but not a secure or compatible assignment workflow. |
+| Pickup and delivery | Client mutations include `orderPickedUp` and `updateOrderStatusRider`; these are absent from backend SDL. The generic backend status mutation can set `PICKED`, `DELIVERED`, or `COMPLETED` directly. | No enforced driver-only delivery sequence. |
+| Completion | `COMPLETED` and timestamps exist in the model; the generic mutation can write the status. No role, assigned-rider, current-state, or transition check protects it. | A status value is writable, not proof of a valid delivery-completion process. |
+
+The stored backend statuses are `PENDING`, `ACCEPTED`, `ASSIGNED`, `PICKED`, `DELIVERED`, `COMPLETED`, and `CANCELLED`. The checked-in clients use the same common strings, but also rely on legacy booleans and timestamp/property names. No canonical enum or transition policy is enforced.
+
+## Authentication, authorization, database, and security
+
+### Authentication and access control
+
+* The backend verifies a JWT, checks that the referenced account still exists and is active, and then puts the role from the token into request context. The role is not refreshed from the loaded database record, so a role change does not invalidate an older token immediately.
+* Role helper functions exist, but they are not consistently applied to the resolvers that need them. `updateOrderStatus` and `assignRider` require only an authenticated context; they permit arbitrary status changes and rider assignment.
+* `getActiveOrders` returns active orders for any authenticated account when no restaurant filter is supplied. It populates customer, restaurant, rider, and food references. `ordersByRestId` requires authentication but does not check that the caller owns the requested restaurant or is an admin.
+* `users` returns every active user to any authenticated caller. The GraphQL `User` type exposes phone and notification-token fields, increasing the impact of broad reads.
+* Order subscriptions accept caller-supplied restaurant/order identifiers without an authorization check. The backend uses in-process `PubSub`; the server starts with Apollo `startStandaloneServer` and does not set up a WebSocket subscription server in the included entry point.
+* The backend's `order(id)` query and some restaurant mutations do perform ownership checks; this does not compensate for the unchecked list/mutation/subscription paths.
+* OTP resolver code stores OTPs and prints them to logs; delivery through email/SMS is marked TODO. The backend password validator and authentication flow have not been tested here.
+
+### Secrets and deployment configuration
+
+* Five tracked Google service-account JSON files contain `private_key` and `client_email` fields: `shita-multivendor-app/google-service-account copy.json`, `shita-multivendor-app/google-service-account.json`, `shita-multivendor-store/google-service-account copy.json`, `shita-multivendor-rider/google-service-account copy.json`, and `shita-multivendor-rider/google-service-account.json`. Their values are intentionally not reproduced. Treat associated credentials as compromised: owners must revoke/rotate them, replace the tracked files with secret-managed configuration, and address Git history before any use.
+* `shita-multivendor-rider/package.json` embeds Sentry upload credential material in a source-control script. Treat it as exposed and rotate it; the value is not reproduced.
+* Backend configuration contains insecure fallback JWT/admin settings, and `shita-multivendor-backend/docker-compose.yml` contains hard-coded Mongo credentials. Do not reuse those values; replace with generated secret-managed values.
+* Terraform creates an AWS PostgreSQL/PostGIS RDS resource, while the checked-in service connects through Mongoose to MongoDB. No adapter or migration connecting these designs is present.
+* MongoDB has no Firebase-style security rules in this repository. Resolver-level authorization is the primary data boundary and is currently incomplete.
+
+### Data handling and security baseline required
+
+Before customer data is introduced: rotate/revoke exposed credentials; purge secret material from Git history; remove all fallbacks and committed credentials; use managed secret storage; enforce role and object ownership on every query, mutation, and subscription; restrict data returned by `User`; validate inputs; add server-side price calculation; enforce order transitions and idempotency; implement and verify payment webhooks; use TLS and restrictive CORS; apply rate limits and safe logging; and test tenant isolation, account revocation, authorization, and data retention.
+
+## Payments, notifications, and REST
+
+* The backend `placeOrder` mutation sets every non-COD payment to `PAID` and copies the client-supplied total into `paidAmount`. There is no payment-provider confirmation in that mutation.
+* Payment packages/configuration and order payment fields are present, but the included backend server source does not register the client-called REST routes below. They may be supplied by a separate/older service, but that runtime was not accessed or verified.
+
+| Client | REST/API route or service referenced in source | Source location | Included backend implementation |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Customer mobile | `stripe/create-checkout-session?id=<orderId>` | `shita-multivendor-app/src/screens/Stripe/StripeCheckout.js:78` | Not found |
+| Customer mobile | `paypal?id=<orderId>` plus `paypal/success` and `paypal/cancel` return URLs | `shita-multivendor-app/src/screens/Paypal/Paypal.js:42,58,67` | Not found |
+| Customer mobile | `hyp/create-hyp-api-sign?id=<orderId>` | `shita-multivendor-app/src/screens/Hyp/HypCheckout.js:166` | Not found |
+| Admin web | `stripe/account` | `shita-multivendor-admin/lib/ui/screen-components/protected/restaurant/payment/main/index.tsx:40` | Not found |
+| Customer web | `stripe/create-checkout-session?id=<orderId>&platform=web` | `shita-multivendor-web/lib/ui/screens/protected/order/checkout/index.tsx:833` | Not found |
+| Customer web | `/api/order-tracking/<orderId>` | `shita-multivendor-web/lib/ui/screen-components/protected/order-tracking/components/tracking-status-wrapper.tsx:15` | No matching Next.js API route was found in the web source |
+| Customer/admin web and mobile | Google Places/geocoding; Cloudinary uploads; country/IP lookup in customer mobile | Client geocoding, upload, and location modules | External providers, not backend routes |
+
+The app clients also call GraphQL over HTTP and configure GraphQL-over-WebSocket URLs. The backend entry point only starts the standalone GraphQL HTTP server; no corresponding REST router or WebSocket subscription server is registered there.
+
+* Customer/admin web service workers contain Firebase background-message handlers. The backend `pushToken` mutation stores a token, but no server-side send/dispatch path using Firebase Admin/APNs was found. A client receiver plus a stored token is not a functioning end-to-end push system.
+* A Firebase Web API key is included as public client configuration in the checked-in web service worker. Its value is not reproduced; restrict it to the intended APIs/referrers and do not use it as authorization.
+* `Subscription` declarations exist, but the backend's standalone entry point does not configure WebSocket subscription transport; PubSub is process-local, not a durable cross-instance event bus.
+
+## Build compatibility, dependency age, and tests
+
+* Backend `Dockerfile` uses `node:18-alpine`, while the declared backend dependencies include Apollo Server 5 and Mongoose 9, which target Node 20 or later. Align the container/runtime with the supported engines or pin compatible dependency majors before considering a build.
+* The backend Docker health check requests `/health`, but the included server entry point does not register that path. A container health check is therefore unverified and may fail even if GraphQL starts.
+* Mobile SDKs are mixed: customer and rider use Expo 53; restaurant uses Expo 54. Customer and rider apps declare React 19 / React Native 0.79.5, while the customer app declares `react-test-renderer` 18.x and the rider app declares `jest-expo` 52 alongside Expo 53. The restaurant app is Expo 54 / React 19.1. Admin and customer web are Next 14.x. These are older major-version lines relative to the review date; update only through a tested compatibility plan.
+* No backend or mobile test source files were found. Backend `test` is a placeholder that prints “Add tests”. Admin/web Cypress content is the starter “todo” test, not product coverage. Store/rider declare Jest scripts but have no test files.
+* No install, lint, test, type-check, or build was run under the read-only/Phase 1A constraints. Build status is therefore **not verified**.
+
+## License and commercial use
+
+The repository root includes an MIT license naming Feras Shita (2023); the admin and customer-web subprojects also include Creative Tim MIT notices (2019). MIT permits commercial use, subject to retaining copyright/license notices and the disclaimer when redistributing substantial portions. A complete dependency, font, image, map, logo, and trademark license audit was not performed; those materials may carry separate terms and must be inventoried for a client delivery.
+
+## Rush33 comparison and recommendation
+
+This comparison uses only the Rush33 facts supplied by the project owner; Rush33 source was not inspected in this phase.
+
+| Requirement | Shita source | Rush33 summary supplied |
+|---|---|---|
+| Customer app | Present | Present |
+| Driver app | Present | Present |
+| Restaurant app/dashboard | Separate restaurant mobile app; also vendor/admin web areas | Restaurant dashboard |
+| Admin dashboard | Present | Missing |
+| Customer web | Present | Not stated |
+| Backend | Apollo GraphQL / Mongoose-Mongo source plus separate AWS/PostgreSQL Terraform scaffold | Firebase |
+| Security position | Serious exposed-credential, resolver authorization, order-pricing, and contract gaps | Significant authentication and order-security problems |
+
+**Recommendation:** Shita is the better *feature-surface starting point* because it already contains separate customer, restaurant, rider, and admin clients plus a customer storefront and Arabic/English assets. It is not the safer production backend: the included API contract is mismatched and its order/security behavior is not ready for customer data. Neither project should be used unchanged for a client’s real customer records or payments. Treat Shita as reusable UI/product source, then select and harden one backend architecture before live data.
+
+## Phase 1A implementation decision
+
+The audit and contract are being prepared as reviewable documentation. **No backend source-code aliases or behavior changes are included.** The mismatch is not a spelling-only issue: the clients disagree on operation names, arguments, order/item/address shapes, and the meaning of `assignOrder`; exposing aliases to the current generic order mutations would preserve or expand unsafe state changes. The smallest safe implementation boundary is to review/approve the canonical contract first, then implement authenticated adapters and transition checks with tests. No deployment or production changes were made.
