+# Proposed canonical API contract
+
+**Status: proposal only.** This document defines one contract for the existing customer mobile, restaurant mobile, rider mobile, admin web, and customer web clients. It is not an implemented or runtime-validated schema. It deliberately keeps the current Apollo GraphQL + Mongoose/MongoDB architecture as the target for this phase; the separate Terraform PostgreSQL stack must not become a second application database without an approved architecture change.
+
+## Contract principles
+
+1. Use one canonical GraphQL endpoint and one set of object/input types for every client.
+2. Derive identity and role from the authenticated account on the server. Clients never submit a trusted `userId`, role, total, paid amount, or order status.
+3. Price orders on the server using current menu, variation, add-on, zone, tax, delivery, and coupon records.
+4. Enforce role, ownership, and state-transition rules in resolvers/services; MongoDB is not protected by Firebase rules.
+5. Return the minimum personal data a caller needs. Never expose password hashes, OTPs, notification tokens, provider secrets, or internal auth/session data through GraphQL.
+6. Keep backward compatibility in explicit adapters while clients are migrated. Do not expose unsafe generic aliases to the current arbitrary-status mutation.
+
+## Canonical roles and enums
+
+```graphql
+enum UserRole {
+  CUSTOMER
+  VENDOR
+  RIDER
+  ADMIN
+}
+
+enum OrderStatus {
+  PENDING
+  ACCEPTED
+  ASSIGNED
+  PICKED_UP
+  DELIVERED
+  COMPLETED
+  CANCELLED
+}
+
+enum PaymentMethod {
+  COD
+  CARD
+  PAYPAL
+  STRIPE
+}
+
+enum PaymentStatus {
+  PENDING
+  AUTHORIZED
+  PAID
+  FAILED
+  REFUNDED
+}
+
+type Money {
+  amountMinor: Int!
+  currency: String!
+}
+```
+
+Use integer minor units (for example, cents/piastres) or a rigorously specified decimal scalar for money; do not accept client-calculated totals as authoritative. `USER` in existing data maps to `CUSTOMER`; existing `PICKED` maps to `PICKED_UP`. Perform that mapping in an explicit compatibility layer and migration plan, not by silently changing stored records.
+
+## Required operation surface
+
+The exact naming can be adjusted before implementation, but clients should converge on this semantic contract:
+
+```graphql
+type Query {
+  currentUser: User
+  restaurants(page: Int, limit: Int, search: String, zoneId: ID): RestaurantConnection!
+  restaurant(id: ID!): Restaurant
+  zones: [Zone!]!
+  food(restaurantId: ID!, categoryId: ID): [Food!]!
+  myAddresses: [Address!]!
+  myOrders(page: Int, limit: Int, status: OrderStatus): OrderConnection!
+  order(id: ID!): Order
+  restaurantOrders(restaurantId: ID!, page: Int, limit: Int, status: OrderStatus): OrderConnection!
+  availableOrders(page: Int, limit: Int): OrderConnection!
+  adminUsers(page: Int, limit: Int, search: String): UserConnection!
+  adminRestaurants(page: Int, limit: Int, search: String): RestaurantConnection!
+  adminRiders(page: Int, limit: Int, search: String, zoneId: ID): UserConnection!
+  adminOrders(page: Int, limit: Int, status: OrderStatus): OrderConnection!
+}
+
+type Mutation {
+  registerCustomer(input: RegisterCustomerInput!): AuthPayload!
+  login(input: LoginInput!): AuthPayload!
+  updateProfile(input: UpdateProfileInput!): User!
+  changePassword(input: ChangePasswordInput!): SuccessMessage!
+  requestPasswordReset(email: String!): SuccessMessage!
+  confirmPasswordReset(input: ConfirmPasswordResetInput!): SuccessMessage!
+
+  createAddress(input: AddressInput!): Address!
+  updateAddress(id: ID!, input: AddressInput!): Address!
+  deleteAddress(id: ID!): SuccessMessage!
+
+  createOrder(input: CreateOrderInput!): Order!
+  acceptOrder(orderId: ID!, preparationMinutes: Int!): Order!
+  rejectOrder(orderId: ID!, reason: String!): Order!
+  cancelOrder(orderId: ID!, reason: String!): Order!
+  assignRider(orderId: ID!, riderId: ID!): Order!
+  claimOrder(orderId: ID!): Order!
+  markOrderPickedUp(orderId: ID!): Order!
+  markOrderDelivered(orderId: ID!): Order!
+  completeOrder(orderId: ID!): Order!
+  setRiderAvailability(available: Boolean!): User!
+  updateRiderLocation(latitude: Float!, longitude: Float!): SuccessMessage!
+
+  createPaymentCheckout(orderId: ID!, provider: PaymentMethod!): PaymentCheckout!
+  adminCreateRestaurant(input: RestaurantInput!): Restaurant!
+  adminUpdateRestaurant(id: ID!, input: RestaurantUpdateInput!): Restaurant!
+  adminCreateRider(input: RiderInput!): User!
+  adminUpdateUserStatus(userId: ID!, active: Boolean!): User!
+}
+
+type Subscription {
+  orderUpdated(orderId: ID!): Order!
+  riderLocationUpdated(orderId: ID!): RiderLocation!
+}
+```
+
+`Connection`, `User`, `Restaurant`, `Food`, `Address`, `Order`, `PaymentCheckout`, and input definitions should be finalized from the actual client fields before schema implementation. The snippet is an operation-surface outline, not a complete executable SDL.
+
+### Order input and server-owned totals
+
+```graphql
+input CreateOrderInput {
+  restaurantId: ID!
+  items: [OrderLineInput!]!
+  addressId: ID
+  deliveryAddress: DeliveryAddressInput
+  couponCode: String
+  paymentMethod: PaymentMethod!
+  tipAmountMinor: Int
+  instructions: String
+}
+
+input OrderLineInput {
+  foodId: ID!
+  variationId: ID
+  optionIds: [ID!]
+  quantity: Int!
+  specialInstructions: String
+}
+```
+
+Exactly one of `addressId` or `deliveryAddress` must be supplied. The server loads the restaurant, menu items, variations, add-on options, zone, current tax/fees, and coupon; it verifies availability and that all items belong to the selected restaurant; then it computes and persists an immutable price snapshot. Creation must be idempotent for a client retry and must not create a duplicate order.
+
+### Order lifecycle and allowed actors
+
+| Current state | Next state | Authorized actor / condition |
+|---|---|---|
+| `PENDING` | `ACCEPTED` | The owner of the order’s restaurant. |
+| `PENDING` | `CANCELLED` | Customer who placed the order, restaurant owner, or admin, subject to cancellation policy. |
+| `ACCEPTED` | `ASSIGNED` | Admin/authorized dispatcher assigns a valid, available rider in the delivery zone; alternatively, an explicitly designed rider claim service assigns the authenticated rider atomically. |
+| `ACCEPTED` | `CANCELLED` | Restaurant owner or admin; customer cancellation only if policy allows. |
+| `ASSIGNED` | `PICKED_UP` | The rider assigned to this order. |
+| `ASSIGNED` | `CANCELLED` | Admin/dispatcher before pickup, with reason and any payment action. |
+| `PICKED_UP` | `DELIVERED` | The rider assigned to this order, with delivery evidence/policy as required. |
+| `DELIVERED` | `COMPLETED` | Customer confirmation or a defined server/admin completion rule. |
+
+`COMPLETED` and `CANCELLED` are terminal. A post-pickup cancellation, reassignment, refund, or correction must be an explicit audited admin workflow, not an arbitrary status write. Every transition should be atomic, timestamped, evented, and rejected when the stored current state does not match the transition’s required state.
+
+### Authorization matrix
+
+| Operation family | Customer | Restaurant owner | Assigned rider | Admin |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Public restaurant/menu browsing | Read active public records | Read active public records | Read active public records | Read |
+| Profile/address | Own record only | Own account only | Own account only | Managed, audited support path |
+| Order detail/history | Own orders only | Own restaurant’s orders | Assigned orders only | Any order, audited |
+| Accept/reject | No | Own restaurant, valid state | No | Override with audit |
+| Rider assignment | No | Only if an approved dispatch policy allows it | Claim only if eligible and unassigned | Assign/reassign |
+| Pickup/delivery status | No | No | Assigned order only, valid next state | Audited override |
+| User/store/rider administration | No | Own store profile only | Own rider profile only | Admin role only |
+| Order/location subscriptions | Own order only | Own restaurant’s orders | Assigned orders only | Admin role only |
+
+Authorization must include both the role check and object-ownership check. A valid JWT alone is not sufficient.
+
+## Payments, notifications, and subscriptions
+
+* `createPaymentCheckout` creates a provider-side checkout/session and returns a redirect/client secret appropriate to the provider. It does not mark the order paid.
+* A signed, idempotent provider webhook is the authority for `AUTHORIZED`, `PAID`, `FAILED`, and `REFUNDED`. The webhook verifies amount, currency, order identity, and provider event replay; the client cannot set these fields.
+* COD remains `PENDING` until the defined cash-collection step is recorded.
+* Push-token registration only stores a token. A separate server-side dispatcher must send notifications, handle provider errors/token expiry, avoid sending sensitive order data, and be covered by tests.
+* Subscriptions require authentication and an ownership filter at subscription setup and event delivery. Use a cross-instance event transport for multi-replica operation; in-memory PubSub is not sufficient.
+
+## Client migration and compatibility
+
+1. Keep the current schema stable while the canonical API is added behind secure services.
+2. Build explicit adapters for old names/arguments only where semantics and ownership can be preserved. Do not map restaurant and rider `assignOrder(id)` to one ambiguous operation.
+3. Migrate each client in this order: customer mobile/web, restaurant, rider, admin; validate each operation against generated schema/types.
+4. Map legacy `orderInput` arrays and status/timestamp names to the canonical order model. Do not accept legacy client totals or payment state.
+5. Remove old compatibility operations only after telemetry confirms no supported client uses them and the data impact has been documented.
+
+## Acceptance checks before implementation is considered complete
+
+* Schema validation succeeds for every operation in `CLIENT_OPERATION_INVENTORY.md`, with each client on the same documented contract.
+* Resolver tests prove customer/vendor/rider/admin isolation for reads, writes, and subscriptions.
+* Order tests cover every allowed and rejected transition, concurrency, retries, item/restaurant membership, pricing, cancellation, and rider reassignment.
+* Payment tests cover success/failure, webhook signature checks, duplicate events, refunds, COD, and client tampering.
+* Build/type-check and end-to-end tests run on the supported Node/Expo toolchains without installing dependencies during this audit phase.
