+# Shita project status — Phase 1A
+
+**Reviewed snapshot:** `b6b751282b843623f9577ae8baf6705247e4d495` (`main`, 2026-08-20).  
+**Review date:** 2026-10-27.  
+**Scope:** Source audit and proposed shared GraphQL contract for the five existing clients.
+
+## Status at the end of Phase 1A
+
+| Area | Status | Evidence / remaining work |
+|---|---|---|
+| Customer Android/iOS app | Exists | Expo/React Native source. Backend contract compatibility is incomplete. |
+| Restaurant app | Exists | Separate Expo/React Native source. Its order acceptance/pickup operations are absent from the included backend SDL. |
+| Rider app | Exists | Separate Expo/React Native source. Its rider login, assignment, and rider-status operations do not match the included backend. |
+| Admin web dashboard | Exists | Next.js source with super-admin/vendor areas. Most admin operations are absent from the included backend. |
+| Customer web storefront | Exists | Next.js source with checkout/tracking flows. Its API operations are not covered by the included backend. |
+| GraphQL backend | Exists, incomplete | Apollo Server + TypeScript + Mongoose/MongoDB. Many declared fields are placeholders; client schemas diverge. |
+| AWS infrastructure | Scaffold only | Terraform describes an AWS/PostgreSQL/PostGIS stack that is not connected to the Mongoose/MongoDB application code. No deployment was inspected. |
+| Order workflow | Partial and unsafe | Order creation and status/assignment writes exist, but store acceptance and rider delivery operations are missing/incompatible and authorization/state transitions are not enforced. |
+| Payments | Not production-functional in included backend | Non-COD orders are marked paid without provider confirmation; client REST routes are absent from this backend source. |
+| Push notifications | Not implemented end-to-end | Token storage exists; no sender/dispatcher path was found. |
+| Arabic/English | Partial source assets | Localization resources exist, but full coverage, RTL behavior, and language QA are unverified. |
+| Commercial license | MIT source license | Commercial use is allowed subject to preserving notices/disclaimer; third-party assets/dependencies require separate review. |
+| Production-data readiness | **Blocked** | Exposed credential material, insecure resolver authorization, client-trusted order prices, contract mismatch, payment/push gaps, and absent tests. |
+
+## Deliverables and changes
+
+* `docs/BACKEND_AUDIT.md` — source findings, security blockers, commercial-use notes, and Rush33 comparison.
+* `docs/API_CONTRACT.md` — proposed canonical GraphQL surface, order lifecycle, authorization matrix, and acceptance checks.
+* `docs/CLIENT_OPERATION_INVENTORY.md` — detected client operation labels, root fields, source locations, and whether each root field is declared in the backend SDL.
+* **No backend source-code changes were made.** The contract mismatch is semantic, not a safe rename-only task. Adding aliases to the current generic status/assignment mutations would not make the clients compatible and could expand access to unsafe order changes. The source repository was not connected to this Replit session; these docs are prepared as reviewable deliverables and were not pushed to GitHub.
+* No dependencies were installed. No credentials were used. No build, test, lint, deployment, production-data operation, or database migration was run.
+
+## Immediate blockers before real customer data
+
+1. Revoke/rotate all committed Google service-account keys and the rider Sentry upload credential. Remove credentials from tracked files and remediate Git history; no revocation or history rewrite has been performed in this phase.
+2. Replace fallback/committed secrets with secret-managed configuration and remove hard-coded deployment credentials.
+3. Choose one database architecture: the included backend uses MongoDB/Mongoose; the Terraform scaffold provisions PostgreSQL/PostGIS.
+4. Enforce current-role checks, least-privilege access, object ownership, and authorized subscriptions throughout every API path.
+5. Make order totals server-owned and implement validated, idempotent order creation and permitted state transitions.
+6. Implement provider-verified payment state and the missing payment routes/webhooks; implement push delivery rather than token storage alone.
+7. Align all five clients with one schema, fix runtime/dependency compatibility, and add tests for authorization, order lifecycle, pricing, concurrency, payment, and notifications.
+8. Complete the third-party asset/license inventory and client-facing privacy/data-retention requirements.
+
+## Comparison and recommended next phase
+
+Using only the Rush33 summary supplied by the project owner, Shita is the better starting point for the requested product surface: it includes customer and driver apps, a separate restaurant app, an admin dashboard, a customer web storefront, and localization assets. Rush33’s supplied report says it has no admin dashboard. Both projects have serious authentication/order-security concerns; the existence of a newer Mongo backend in Shita does not make that backend safe or compatible.
+
+**Recommended direction:** retain Shita as a potential front-end/product-surface base, but do not onboard real data or payments. First approve the canonical contract and chosen database/deployment architecture; then implement the secured backend workflow and adapters in a separate, reviewable phase. Do not continue to Phase 1B until the owner has reviewed and approved this Phase 1A result.
